# Paths the PR reviewer does not read (SERV-87).
#
# One extended regex per line, matched against the repository-relative path of
# every changed file. `#` comments and blank lines are ignored.
#
# Per-repo on purpose even though the workflow is shared (SERV-92): which
# artifacts are generated is a fact about this repository. The shared workflow
# reads this from the BASE ref of the PR under review, so widening it in a PR
# does not exempt that PR — and `.github/review-ignore` is itself in this
# repo's `sensitive_paths`, so a PR that widens it escalates.
#
# What belongs here is regenerated artifacts, not merely large files. The test
# is: if this file is wrong, is the fix to edit it? For a generated file the
# answer is no — you re-run the generator — so a review comment on its diff has
# nowhere to land. A large hand-written file is exactly what the reviewer should
# be spending its budget on.

# Generated by scripts/gen-schema.sh: the schema you get by applying every
# migration in order to an empty database. The `migration staleness guard` job
# in ci.yml regenerates it and byte-compares, so it cannot drift from the
# migrations without CI going red.
#
# There is a real counter-argument and it does not win, so record why. This
# artifact keeps PRIVILEGES deliberately (gen-schema.sh says so at the pg_dump
# call) BECAUSE the tier grants are how CLAUDE.md's first invariant is enforced
# — a loosened grant is meant to surface as a schema diff. That makes schema.sql
# look like the best place to SEE a tier regression.
#
# It is not, because the reviewer already reads the cause. A grant only changes
# via a `.up.sql`, `migrations/` is in `sensitive_paths`, and the migration is
# the authoritative and far shorter statement of the same fact. Excluding the
# rendered copy costs the review nothing and buys back the budget that ~200
# lines of pg_dump per migration would otherwise eat — budget better spent on
# the SQL that caused it. The staleness guard, not the reviewer, is what stops
# the two disagreeing.
^schema\.sql$

# Generated by scripts/gen-asrclient.sh from deploy/asr/openapi.yaml: the Go
# client the contract describes. The `ASR client staleness guard` step in
# ci.yml regenerates it to a temp file and byte-compares, and verify.sh runs
# the same check, so it cannot drift from the spec without CI going red.
#
# This is exactly the test this file states for itself: if the generated client
# is wrong, the fix is to change deploy/asr/openapi.yaml and re-run the
# generator, so a review comment on this diff has nowhere to land. The SPEC is
# where the review belongs, and it is under `deploy/`, which is already in
# `sensitive_paths` -- so excluding the rendered copy moves budget onto the
# hand-written file rather than away from the subject.
^internal/asrclient/client\.gen\.go$

# release-please owns this and rewrites it wholesale on every release. Live as
# of CHRN-73, which wired `release-please-config.json` and
# `.release-please-manifest.json`; the entry was added prospectively in CHRN-72
# and this comment is corrected rather than left claiming the train is unbuilt.
#
# It is also `.md`, so the docs-only rule already covers it on a release PR;
# this covers the case where it rides along with code.
#
# Note the shared workflow derives a release PR's expected file set from
# `release-please-config.json` on the BASE ref, so adding an `extra-files`
# target there cannot silently un-match the release skip.
^CHANGELOG\.md$

# NOT excluded, deliberately, and each for a reason:
#
#   go.sum          — a dependency tree, but also the checksum database. A line
#                     changing here without a matching go.mod change is the
#                     shape of a supply-chain problem, and that is worth a read.
#                     It is not in `sensitive_paths` either: worth reading,
#                     not worth the expensive tier on its own.
#   migrations/     — hand-written SQL, applied at boot, fatal on failure. In
#                     `sensitive_paths`.
#   docs/salvage/   — recovered vox-dictate sources. Not generated: they are the
#                     salvaged input CLAUDE.md tells you to read before
#                     re-deriving anything. The docs-only rule already skips a
#                     PR that touches nothing else, which is the right handling
#                     and a different one — that rule yields a SKIPPED check
#                     saying "documentation only", where this file would silently
#                     drop the paths from a review that still ran.
#   deploy/Dockerfile, verify.sh, scripts/gen-schema.sh
#                   — hand-written build and verification definitions, all three
#                     in `sensitive_paths`.
#   deploy/asr/openapi.yaml
#                   — the CONTRACT, hand-written, and the thing two services in
#                     two languages generate from. Under `deploy/`, so already
#                     in `sensitive_paths`; named here so that nobody excluding
#                     "the OpenAPI stuff" takes the source out with the artefact.
#   internal/asrclient/oapi-codegen.yaml
#                   — the generator's flags, which are themselves an artefact
#                     nothing regenerates.
